    {
      "id": "Jhdp--Kc0sXKy1buv57IC",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -11654.011239110485,
      "y": -50170.65557384006,
      "width": 2038.6666259765625,
      "height": 950,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b7t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 841371064,
      "version": 5430,
      "versionNonce": 1301591991,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1780048407344,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The goal:\nA \"job system\" as described in the talk \" Parallelizing the Naughty Dog Engine Using Fibers\", but aims to add work stealing.\n\n- Jobs are synchronized by waiting for atomic counters only, which are decremented when other jobs finish\n- This forms, effectively, a graph or tree of dependent jobs that are scheduled in parallel across cores using fibres\n    - fibres are a threads context and stack and are like threads but scheduled in user space. They are similar to coroutines.\n        - context consists of instruction ptr, stack ptr and callee saved registers \n    - Context switches are cheap and with scheduling you can allow jobs to efficiently wait for other jobs to complete, maximizing usage of CPU cores\n        - when they wait they are placed into a wait list, the thread context switches back to the scheduler thread and dequeues more work to do while the old \n        fibre waits for its child tasks\n        - switching fibre context must be implemented in assembly or use an OS's API, If done in assembly it needs to observe proper ABI compatibility\n            - there are libraries such as boost context\n            - Windows has a fibre API, I don't think linux does\n\n- The goal is for maximum parallelization of jobs, simplicity of use, and maximum utilization of CPU cores\n\n- thread safe data queues are lock free using atomic ints\n\n- To the system described in the naughty dog talk I want to add work stealing, this should level the work across CPUs more efficiently\n    - I think their system has a single fibre pool, mine has one per thread, but as a fibre can start on one thread\n    and finish on another, this fibre then can't be deallocated. I am thinking that we can try and just don't deallocate them and the fibre pools should be reset each frame OR a global one used\n    and suffer contention for the \"lock\". Stealing should hopefully be quite rare if a large amount of jobs are fed into the job system. The priorities are arranged such that dequeueing work from a threads\n    own local queue is the most preferred queue and stealing is the least.\n    - it would be more cache efficient to reuse the fibres and their stacks so a global pool might be best\n\n- I envision it making more use of normal OS threads than their one did (I think):\n    - there will be a dedicated rendering thread\n        - In parallel to the update happening this will be calling the rendering API functions to render the PREVIOUS frame - double buffered render data\n            - the job system based update step will perform culling and preparation of rendering data, the rendering thread will actually render the data using the rendering API\n    - a dedicated \"platform\" thread polling input and orchestrating the frame\n        - it will poll input, kick off the tree of tasks to the job system to perform the game simulation update, wait for the counter and then synchronize with the render thread, flipping the render data buffer\n    - a dedicated audio thread\n    - OS threads used for IO, as described in the talk\n\n\n- As well as hopefully being fast and efficient it should be capable of providing good and fine grained performance data because it executes functions with a wrapper, and all this comprises the entire update\n    - each worker will log to its own circular buffer and save to their own file at the point in the frame where the update has finished to a file on demand \n    - some tool can then be written to visualize the data ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The goal:\nA \"job system\" as described in the talk \" Parallelizing the Naughty Dog Engine Using Fibers\", but aims to add work stealing.\n\n- Jobs are synchronized by waiting for atomic counters only, which are decremented when other jobs finish\n- This forms, effectively, a graph or tree of dependent jobs that are scheduled in parallel across cores using fibres\n    - fibres are a threads context and stack and are like threads but scheduled in user space. They are similar to coroutines.\n        - context consists of instruction ptr, stack ptr and callee saved registers \n    - Context switches are cheap and with scheduling you can allow jobs to efficiently wait for other jobs to complete, maximizing usage of CPU cores\n        - when they wait they are placed into a wait list, the thread context switches back to the scheduler thread and dequeues more work to do while the old \n        fibre waits for its child tasks\n        - switching fibre context must be implemented in assembly or use an OS's API, If done in assembly it needs to observe proper ABI compatibility\n            - there are libraries such as boost context\n            - Windows has a fibre API, I don't think linux does\n\n- The goal is for maximum parallelization of jobs, simplicity of use, and maximum utilization of CPU cores\n\n- thread safe data queues are lock free using atomic ints\n\n- To the system described in the naughty dog talk I want to add work stealing, this should level the work across CPUs more efficiently\n    - I think their system has a single fibre pool, mine has one per thread, but as a fibre can start on one thread\n    and finish on another, this fibre then can't be deallocated. I am thinking that we can try and just don't deallocate them and the fibre pools should be reset each frame OR a global one used\n    and suffer contention for the \"lock\". Stealing should hopefully be quite rare if a large amount of jobs are fed into the job system. The priorities are arranged such that dequeueing work from a threads\n    own local queue is the most preferred queue and stealing is the least.\n    - it would be more cache efficient to reuse the fibres and their stacks so a global pool might be best\n\n- I envision it making more use of normal OS threads than their one did (I think):\n    - there will be a dedicated rendering thread\n        - In parallel to the update happening this will be calling the rendering API functions to render the PREVIOUS frame - double buffered render data\n            - the job system based update step will perform culling and preparation of rendering data, the rendering thread will actually render the data using the rendering API\n    - a dedicated \"platform\" thread polling input and orchestrating the frame\n        - it will poll input, kick off the tree of tasks to the job system to perform the game simulation update, wait for the counter and then synchronize with the render thread, flipping the render data buffer\n    - a dedicated audio thread\n    - OS threads used for IO, as described in the talk\n\n\n- As well as hopefully being fast and efficient it should be capable of providing good and fine grained performance data because it executes functions with a wrapper, and all this comprises the entire update\n    - each worker will log to its own circular buffer and save to their own file at the point in the frame where the update has finished to a file on demand \n    - some tool can then be written to visualize the data ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
